# What git carries here is the engine and the recipes. Everything else in these
# folders is either something the generator handed back or something a tool can
# make again in seconds, and neither belongs in history.

.venv/
__pycache__/
.DS_Store

# work/ holds each variant's own code and docs, so it is carried. What is
# ignored is what a tool makes inside it. This was the other way round once -
# work/ was excluded wholesale, from when it really was scratch - and the
# reorganisation that moved every variant's source in there recorded the lot as
# deleted. Nothing was lost, but for an afternoon the repository held the engine
# and nothing else.
*_silent.mp4
*_pops.wav
*_hits.wav
*_cues.txt
*_protocol.txt
*_finale.txt
*_locks.wav
*_chord.wav
*_riser.wav
*_hacks.txt
*_act2.json
*_act2.wav
*_preview.mp4

# The captioned poster, under each variant's name for it. Derived twice over -
# add_labels.py writes <topic>_labelled.png from <topic>_poster.jpeg, and
# caption_glass.py writes <topic>_glass.png from that and the clean copy - and
# both commands are in the variants' flow.md. 15 MB of Exercise's alone. What is
# tracked is the jpeg neither command can remake.
*_glass.png
*_sheet.png

# the generated posters, and the finished videos
#
# THE POSTER IS THE ONE ARTEFACT NO COMMAND CAN REMAKE. A base is rebuilt from
# recolor_base and a palette, a labelled poster from add_labels, a clip from
# render.sh - but an image model cannot be asked twice for the same picture, and
# the wave in a poster is the wave that clip's animation was measured against.
# Micro measured the exposure on 9 September: fourteen of its topics existed only
# as untracked files, and a lost work/ would have ended them.
#
# So one archival copy per shipped topic is tracked, named <topic>_poster.jpeg or
# <topic>_poster.png. That is the same trade this repository already makes for
# engine/sfx, where 47 MB of source recordings are carried so a bed can be
# re-cut - and a poster is the harder case, because a recording can be downloaded
# again and a generation cannot.
#
# PREFER THE JPEG. Measured across the four variants: 31 topic posters as jpeg
# average 1.7 MB and total 52.7; the seven that were converted to _poster.png
# average 3.5. Twice the size for not one bit more information, because a png of
# a jpeg is a lossless copy of a lossy original - the conversion exists because
# downstream tools want a png, not because the png is the better record.
#
# The browser-named downloads stay ignored: they are the same picture under a
# name nobody chose, and Micro/work/PROVENANCE.md records which belongs to which.
*.jpeg
*.jpg
!*_poster.png
!*_poster.jpeg
*.mp4
OUTPUT/
/OUTPUT/
INPUT/

# derived: base_<topic>.png and its two companions come out of one
# recolor_base.py call whose palette is written down in Prompts.txt
base_*.png
*_clean.png
*_labelled.png
*_layout.json

# except the engine's own, which are authored and cannot be derived from anything
!engine/base_layer.png
!engine/ribbon_mask.png
!engine/ribbon_rgba.png
!engine/source_wave_poster.jpeg

# checkpoints are commits now. The folders stay on disk; only their reasoning
# is worth carrying, and the copies of the engine inside them are not
CHECKPOINT_*/*
!CHECKPOINT_*/README.md
OldCheckpoints/*/*
!OldCheckpoints/*/README.md

# archive/ checkpoints each carry a copy of the engine's authored wave source.
# Un-ignoring *_poster.jpeg on 8 September - so that a shipped poster could
# never again be ignored by name - exposed six of them, 13 MB, and all six are
# byte-identical to engine/source_wave_poster.jpeg (9545a1a6999c), which is
# tracked. The un-ignore exists to protect artefacts no command can remake;
# this file is not one of them, because it is already in the repository.
archive/**/source_wave_poster.jpeg
